In-Reply-To: <KcCb6KtpPfo.E2HBeCWkJkEJ@davis-rug.groups.google.com>
References: <KcCb6KtpPfo.VRPboHFWlbYJ@davis-rug.groups.google.com> <KcCb6KtpPfo.E2HBeCWkJkEJ@davis-rug.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/davis-rug/c/KcCb6KtpPfo/m/bEaWgSPzXmEJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

SQLite with RSQLite is your best option — the join with merge() is too much for your memory, but your data are quite small.

RSQLite has helpful convenience functions to make this easy: see the manual, but you can load data into SQLite with dbWriteTable(). Then, it's best if you index the columns you'll be joining on (e.g. your foreign keys <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foreign_key>). So following the example on page 19 of the RSQLite manual, this would look like:

dbGetQuery(con, "create index row_name_idx on USArrests (row_names)")

Load and index your other table, and then do an inner join, which would look something like:

dbGetQuery(con, "SELECT * FROM USArrests, USArrests_2 WHERE USArrests.row_names = USArrests_2.row_names limit 5")

This syntax is usually the easiest for people at first, but you could also use INNER JOIN, or NATURAL JOIN (you probably want a natural join if your merge didn't have by.x= and by.y= specified, this is what merge() does).

HTH,
Vince

