Great idea. I'm excited to see the compilation. Obviously, Google is your best friend. For all my even remotely common issues, there's a stackoverflow discussion. I find copying code to a temporary script and working through it while referring back to the discussion to understand what's going on pretty reliably good instruction. I found Richard's book examples tremendously useful for learning how to work with vectors and apply functions, write functions, make more advanced plots, etc. Introduction to Statistical Learning (also available for free online <http://www-bcf.usc.edu/~gareth/ISL/getbook.html>) also teaches through R examples -- I find that a very good way to learn both the statistical principles and the coding.

--
